   "text": {
    "L1": "Seasons come from Earth's tilted axis, not from changing distance from the Sun.",
    "L2": "A common myth says that summer happens when Earth is closer to the Sun. That is not true.",
    "L3": "Earth's axis is tilted about 23.5 degrees, and it keeps pointing the same way as Earth goes around the Sun.",
    "L4": "Around June, the northern half is tilted towards the Sun. Rays are more direct and days are longer, so it is summer in the north.",
    "L5": "Around December, the northern half tilts away. Rays slant and spread out, and days are shorter, so it is winter in the north.",
    "L6": "The southern hemisphere is tilted the opposite way, so its seasons are opposite. When it is summer in India, it is winter in Australia.",
    "L7": "Distance cannot explain this: Earth is closest to the Sun in early January, during northern winter.",
    "L8": "So tilt changes the angle of sunlight and the length of daylight. Now check the model."
   },
